# Decimal Numbers in JavaScript

## Supported Bases and Formats

Decimal numbers can be represented as strings in any base from binary (base 2) up to hexadecimal (base 16). In addition, the following formats are supported:

| Format | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| **`0`**       | One or more digits followed by an optional decimal point and zero or more digits.<br>`0.0`, `000.00000`, `1000.0000`<br>Examples:<ul><li>`000.0000`</li></ul>
| **`.0`**     | A single digit preceded by a dot and an optional decimal point and one or more digits.<br>`0.0`, `.00000`, `.0`<br>Examples:<ul><li>`000.0000`</li></ul>
| **`.`**      | A single dot without any preceding digits or digits after it.<br>.<br>Example: `<blank>`
| **`+.`**     | A plus sign preceding the dot.<br>+.<br>Example: `<blank>`
| **`-.`**     | A minus sign preceding the dot.<br>-.<br>Example: `-0.<blank>`
| **`--`**     | Two consecutive minuses signs.<br>--<br>Example: `--0.<blank>`
| **`...`**    | Three consecutive dots.<br>...<br>Example: `--0..<blank>`
| **`..`**     | Two consecutive dots.<br>..<br>Example: `0..<blank>`
| **`+-`**     | Plus sign immediately before the dot and a minus sign.<br>+.<br>Example: `0.+<blank>`
| **`-.+`**    | Minus sign immediately before the dot and a plus sign.<br>-.<br>Example: `0.-+pytest numeric_edge_cases.py --cov=decimal
